Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:01.839
We love Patterns

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:08.158
We love it when things fall into place when the last piece of the puzzle fits in when a piece of [music] returns

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.390
triumphantly to the Tonic

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:16.619
But we also love to be surprised. We'd love to see the rules get broken for the pattern to shift beneath our feet

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:20.820
For the puzzle to refuse to fit together until we look at it from a new angle

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:29.429
This tension between Pattern and surprise keeps our ears pricked up it gives us enough to hold on to but keeps us on our toes

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:33.239
in both Mathematics and music a

00:00:33.609 --> 00:00:35.729
classic kind of Pattern comes from symmetry

00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:40.739
We say something is symmetric when there is some way we can transform it that keeps it the same

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.489
We look about the same if you flip our image in a mirror

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:48.689
But some of our more distant relatives look the same if you rotate them

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.748
repeating patterns like these intricate Moorish mosaics from the Alhambra in Spain

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:59.459
Stay the same if we shift them over or if we rotate them

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:05.010
The Dodecahedron one of plato's favorite things stays the same if we rotate it

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.209
There are symmetries in higher dimensions as well like this four-dimensional hypercube

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:18.029
The mathematician Emmy nutter discovered that many of the laws of physics are actually symmetries of the universe

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.749
There's another kind of symmetry in mathematics

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.980
Fractals are things that look the same when we [zoom] in on them where each part looks like a smaller version of the [hole]

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.620
They show up in all sorts of places including

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:38.120
ferns

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:42.669
coastlines and this broccoli my daughter brought home from the grocery store [even]

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:51.129
[simple] Mathematics can make fractals take this triangle of Honeycomb and put ones on the outside

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.589
Fill in each cell with the sum of the two numbers above it one plus one equals two

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.619
One plus two equals three and so on

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.119
now color the odd numbers black and the even numbers white

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.619
what do we get if

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:08.979
We continue the pattern and zoom out

00:02:08.979 --> 00:02:14.949
We see [a] triangle made of three triangles and each one is a smaller version of the original

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.989
perhaps the most famous fractal is the Mandelbrot set

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:29.169
Zooming in on different areas reveals endless variety, but always with a smaller copy of the entire set hiding deep inside it

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.660
A

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.950
Musical fractal is a piece where the theme harmonizes with a slowed down version of itself?

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.520
That way the small-scale structure of the piece is

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.189
Repeated at a larger grander scale

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.749
Johann Sebastian bach love to play [with] symmetry in

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:35.120
His [fugues] the theme is repeated many times in different forms in

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.920
The fugue we're going to perform tonight listen for this theme in the horn

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.110
Now listen for the upside down version of it in the second trumpet

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.069
The slowed down version it in the first trumpet

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.969
And the extremely slow down version in the Tuba

